WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.846 --> 00:00:04.283
Scrum is a framework for a small team and complex products:

2
00:00:04.422 --> 00:00:05.874
the best-known agile way.

3
00:00:06.859 --> 00:00:10.911
The Scrum Guide by Ken Schwaber and Jeff Sutherland describes it,

4
00:00:11.108 --> 00:00:12.768
in about thirteen pages.

5
00:00:13.771 --> 00:00:16.406
Fixed accountabilities, events and lists;

6
00:00:16.522 --> 00:00:18.972
the rest it deliberately leaves to the team.

7
00:00:20.109 --> 00:00:24.661
Work runs in sprints of a month or less, often two weeks, back to back.

8
00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.060
Each sprint delivers something usable, in a fixed rhythm.

9
00:00:30.084 --> 00:00:33.962
Longer is risky: the goal goes stale, problems stay hidden.

10
00:00:35.309 --> 00:00:39.186
The Scrum Team is small: typically ten or fewer, with no hierarchy.

11
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.301
It holds three accountabilities.

12
00:00:43.034 --> 00:00:46.494
Since 2020, they are not job titles.

13
00:00:47.502 --> 00:00:52.553
The Product Owner is one person, not a committee, guarding value: what comes first,

14
00:00:52.785 --> 00:00:53.377
and why.

15
00:00:54.369 --> 00:00:58.850
The Scrum Master helps people use Scrum well and removes impediments.

16
00:00:59.552 --> 00:01:00.423
Not the boss.

17
00:01:01.444 --> 00:01:06.112
The Developers do the work: anyone who builds, testers and analysts too.

18
00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:10.618
In Lean terms: the Product Owner is the customer's voice,

19
00:01:10.792 --> 00:01:12.905
the Scrum Master a coach of the system.

20
00:01:14.252 --> 00:01:21.125
In factories, mostly IT teams work this way: MES, ERP, data and machine software.

21
00:01:22.138 --> 00:01:26.387
Common mistake: the IT manager as Product Owner and Scrum Master.

22
00:01:27.124 --> 00:01:28.819
Nobody holds up a mirror.

23
00:01:29.882 --> 00:01:33.656
Ask: who decides what comes first, and who guards how we work?

24
00:01:34.701 --> 00:01:37.592
Example: a pipe maker, a team of seven.

25
00:01:38.306 --> 00:01:41.661
Product Owner: the production manager, two days a week.

26
00:01:42.744 --> 00:01:46.041
Scrum Master: a process improver from the Lean team.

27
00:01:46.707 --> 00:01:48.054
Plus five Developers.

28
00:01:49.058 --> 00:01:54.051
Sprint five's goal: operators report scrap by cause on a screen, not paper.

29
00:01:55.069 --> 00:01:57.449
After two weeks, all shifts use it.

30
00:01:58.208 --> 00:02:00.228
Sprint six starts the next Monday.

31
00:02:01.625 --> 00:02:05.909
Scrum has five events: fixed moments to plan, inspect and adapt.

32
00:02:06.673 --> 00:02:08.705
The sprint contains the other four.

33
00:02:09.698 --> 00:02:12.286
Each has a timebox, a maximum duration.

34
00:02:13.004 --> 00:02:17.659
For a month: planning at most eight hours, the Daily Scrum fifteen minutes.

35
00:02:18.669 --> 00:02:21.142
Sprint Planning answers three questions.

36
00:02:21.852 --> 00:02:23.373
Why is this sprint valuable?

37
00:02:24.128 --> 00:02:25.811
That becomes the sprint goal.

38
00:02:26.839 --> 00:02:27.652
What can be done?

39
00:02:28.349 --> 00:02:29.615
The Developers pick it.

40
00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:31.126
And how?

41
00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:34.202
In pieces of usually a day or less.

42
00:02:35.199 --> 00:02:37.184
The Developers decide how much.

43
00:02:37.902 --> 00:02:40.212
The Product Owner says what matters most.

44
00:02:41.579 --> 00:02:45.666
The Daily Scrum: fifteen minutes for the Developers, every working day,

45
00:02:45.759 --> 00:02:48.546
same time and place, toward the sprint goal.

46
00:02:49.582 --> 00:02:53.286
Not a status meeting for a manager; the team picks the format.

47
00:02:54.327 --> 00:02:57.067
The Sprint Review shows stakeholders what is done;

48
00:02:57.195 --> 00:02:59.656
together they discuss what matters most now.

49
00:03:00.363 --> 00:03:02.778
A working session, not a presentation.

50
00:03:03.828 --> 00:03:07.137
Never a gate: what is done may go into use earlier.

51
00:03:08.161 --> 00:03:12.457
In the Sprint Retrospective the team looks back: Scrum's kaizen engine,

52
00:03:12.666 --> 00:03:14.187
with one concrete action.

53
00:03:15.333 --> 00:03:19.245
Refinement is not an event: refining the list runs all the time.

54
00:03:20.507 --> 00:03:22.899
A beverage maker with ten-day sprints.

55
00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:26.597
The team halves the timeboxes, a common rule of thumb.

56
00:03:27.689 --> 00:03:30.499
Monday, the planning, at most four hours.

57
00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:34.237
Every working day at nine, the Daily Scrum.

58
00:03:35.291 --> 00:03:40.028
Friday of week two, the review with the shift leaders, then the retrospective.

59
00:03:41.022 --> 00:03:45.492
Ten days of fifteen minutes: two and a half hours per person per sprint.

60
00:03:46.851 --> 00:03:48.024
Three artefacts.

61
00:03:48.734 --> 00:03:51.811
Since 2020, each has a commitment.

62
00:03:52.865 --> 00:03:55.048
The Product Backlog is the ordered list of

63
00:03:55.129 --> 00:03:57.277
everything that could improve the product.

64
00:03:58.021 --> 00:04:00.018
The Product Owner decides the order.

65
00:04:01.062 --> 00:04:04.708
Its commitment: the Product Goal, the longer-term objective.

66
00:04:05.728 --> 00:04:09.037
The Sprint Backlog is the Developers' plan for this sprint.

67
00:04:09.816 --> 00:04:11.465
It changes as they learn.

68
00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:17.009
Its commitment: the Sprint Goal, the single objective, which gives focus.

69
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:18.453
Too big?

70
00:04:19.219 --> 00:04:23.224
They renegotiate the scope with the Product Owner; the goal stands.

71
00:04:24.553 --> 00:04:28.756
The Increment is working product that is done; a sprint can have several.

72
00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:33.788
Its commitment: the Definition of Done, when work is really done,

73
00:04:33.869 --> 00:04:35.622
such as tested and installed.

74
00:04:36.717 --> 00:04:40.363
Work that fails it is not shown: back to the Product Backlog.

75
00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:43.848
A quality gate at the end of the line.

76
00:04:44.555 --> 00:04:46.308
Half-done work doesn't count.

77
00:04:47.352 --> 00:04:48.606
Sprint Goal obsolete?

78
00:04:49.339 --> 00:04:52.021
Only the Product Owner may cancel the sprint.

79
00:04:52.768 --> 00:04:53.198
Rarely.

80
00:04:54.198 --> 00:04:57.728
Mistakes: four hundred items, the manager's task list,

81
00:04:57.902 --> 00:04:59.957
a Definition of Done of just code.

82
00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:02.889
So ask: what is the Product Goal?

83
00:05:03.588 --> 00:05:04.552
This sprint's goal?

84
00:05:05.254 --> 00:05:06.961
May I see your Definition of Done?

85
00:05:08.305 --> 00:05:11.509
Linking a meat plant's scales to the MES.

86
00:05:12.269 --> 00:05:15.241
Sprint four's goal: line one sends weights.

87
00:05:16.232 --> 00:05:20.597
The Definition of Done: tested with real weighing data, checked by quality,

88
00:05:20.841 --> 00:05:22.745
on the acceptance environment.

89
00:05:23.783 --> 00:05:25.942
Of five items, three meet it.

90
00:05:26.698 --> 00:05:29.914
Item four is not checked, item five not started.

91
00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:32.157
What does the team show?

92
00:05:32.865 --> 00:05:33.933
Only those three.

93
00:05:34.924 --> 00:05:39.707
Four and five go back to the Product Backlog; the Product Owner decides the order.

94
00:05:40.697 --> 00:05:42.636
Do line one's weights get through?

95
00:05:43.367 --> 00:05:46.838
Then the goal is met: a sprint goal beats a task list.
